   "section": "Overview",
   "title": "Overview",
   "text": "CROSSFADE is a music streaming service for independent artists in which the catalogue is indexed by where a recording sits between genres rather than by a single genre label. Artists upload their own recordings directly and grant the service a streaming licence, which removes the need to negotiate rights with established labels and makes the catalogue consist almost entirely of new and obscure material. Every track is passed through a machine learning scoring model at the moment of ingest; the model returns a probability distribution across a fixed set of genres together with measures of how uncertain and how internally inconsistent that distribution is. Listeners do not browse by genre name. They create stations that describe a target blend, for example sixty per cent jazz and forty per cent hip hop, together with a permitted range of ambiguity, and the service returns recordings that genuinely occupy that region. Because a recording is placed from its audio alone, a track uploaded an hour ago is as recommendable as one with a million plays, which is the operational advantage the service is built around. The database must therefore manage the artist roster and its catalogue, the versioned scoring models, the output each model produced for each track and the probability it assigned to each genre, listener accounts and the stations they define, and a record of every play for the dual purpose of learning listener taste and paying artists their per stream royalty."
